Title: How do I merge two dictionaries in a single expression in Python?.
Body: <p>I want to merge two dictionaries into a new dictionary.</p>
<pre><code>x = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}
y = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}
z = merge(x, y)

&gt;&gt;&gt; z
{'a': 1, 'b': 3, 'c': 4}
</code></pre>
<p>Whenever a key <code>k</code> is present in both dictionaries, only the value <code>y[k]</code> should be kept.</p>

Answers:
You can use the `update()` method of dictionaries to merge two dictionaries into a new dictionary. The `update()` method takes another dictionary as an argument and updates the current dictionary with the key-value pairs from the argument dictionary. If a key is present in both dictionaries, the value from the argument dictionary is used. Here's the code to merge two dictionaries into a new dictionary:
<pre><code>x = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}
y = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}
z = {}
z.update(x)
z.update(y)

&gt;&gt;&gt; z
{'a': 1, 'b': 3, 'c': 4}
</code></pre>
This code creates a new empty dictionary `z`, updates it with the key-value pairs from `x` using the `update()` method, and then updates it with the key-value pairs from `y`. The resulting dictionary `z` contains all the key-value pairs from `x` and `y`, with the values from `y` taking precedence in case of key conflicts.
You can also use the `**` operator to merge two dictionaries into a new dictionary:
<pre><code>x = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}
y = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}
z = {**x, **y}

&gt;&gt;&gt; z
{'a': 1, 'b': 3, 'c': 4}
</code></pre>
This code creates a new dictionary `z` by unpacking the key-value pairs from `x` and `y` using the `**` operator. The resulting dictionary `z` contains all the key-value pairs from `x` and `y`, with the values from `y` taking precedence in case of key conflicts.
You can also use the `dict()` function to merge two dictionaries into a new dictionary:
<pre><code>x = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}
y = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}
z = dict(x, **y)

&gt;&gt;&gt; z
{'a': 1, 'b': 3, 'c': 4}
</code></pre>
This code creates a new dictionary `z` by passing the key-value pairs from `x` to the `dict()` function, and then passing the key-value pairs from `y` using the `**` operator. The resulting dictionary `z` contains all the key-value pairs from `x` and `y`, with the values from `y` taking precedence in case of key conflicts.
You can also use the `ChainMap()` class from the `collections` module to merge two dictionaries into a new dictionary:
<pre><code>x = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}
y = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}
from collections import ChainMap
z = dict(ChainMap(y, x))

&gt;&gt;&gt; z
{'a': 1, 'b': 3, 'c': 4}
</code></pre>
This code creates a new dictionary `z` by passing the key-value pairs from `y` and `x` to the `ChainMap()` class constructor, and then passing the resulting object to the `dict()` function. The resulting dictionary `z` contains all the key-value pairs from `x` and `y`, with the values from `y` taking precedence in case of key conflicts.
