Title: How do I check if an array includes a value in JavaScript?.
Body: <p>What is the most concise and efficient way to find out if a JavaScript array contains a value?</p>
<p>This is the only way I know to do it:</p>
<pre><code>function contains(a, obj) {
    for (var i = 0; i &lt; a.length; i++) {
        if (a[i] === obj) {
            return true;
        }
    }
    return false;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Is there a better and more concise way to accomplish this?</p>
<p>This is very closely related to Stack Overflow question <em><a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/143847/best-way-to-find-an-item-in-a-javascript-array">Best way to find an item in a JavaScript Array?</a></em> which addresses finding objects in an array using <code>indexOf</code>.</p>

Answers:
None. Here is a simple JavaScript implementation of the `insert` method:

```javascript
Array.prototype.insert = function(index, item) {
  this.splice(index, 0, item);
};
```

This code adds the `insert` method to the `Array` prototype, so you can use it like this:

```javascript
var arr = [1, 2, 3, 4];
arr.insert(2, 'inserted item');
console.log(arr); // Output: [1, 2, 'inserted item', 3, 4]
```

This code works by calling the `splice` method on the array, which inserts the item at the specified index. The second argument to `splice` specifies the number of items to remove (in this case, 0, so no items are removed).

You can also use the `insert` method in jQuery, like this:

```javascript
var arr = [1, 2, 3, 4];
$.each(arr, function(index, value) {
  if (index === 2) {
    arr.insert(index, 'inserted item');
  }
});
console.log(arr); // Output: [1, 2, 'inserted item', 3, 4]
```

This code uses the `$.each` method to loop through the array, and inserts the item at the specified index when the index matches 2. Note that this code assumes you have included the jQuery library.

