Title: How can I add new array elements at the beginning of an array in JavaScript?.
Body: <p>I have a need to add or prepend elements at the beginning of an array.</p>
<p>For example, if my array looks like below:</p>
<pre><code>[23, 45, 12, 67]
</code></pre>
<p>And the response from my AJAX call is <code>34</code>, I want the updated array to be like the following:</p>
<pre><code>[34, 23, 45, 12, 67]
</code></pre>
<p>Currently I am planning to do it like this:</p>
<pre><code>var newArray = [];
newArray.push(response);

for (var i = 0; i &lt; theArray.length; i++) {
    newArray.push(theArray[i]);
}

theArray = newArray;
delete newArray;
</code></pre>
<p>Is there a better way to do this? Does JavaScript have any built-in functionality that does this?</p>
<p>The complexity of my method is <code>O(n)</code> and it would be really interesting to see better implementations.</p>

Answers:
Certainly! JavaScript provides a straightforward way to add elements to an array at the beginning without needing to manually copy all existing elements. You can use the `unshift` method, which adds one or more elements to the start of an array and returns the new length of the array. Here’s how you can achieve this:

```javascript
// Example array
let theArray = [23, 45, 12, 67];

// New element to prepend
let response = 34;

// Use unshift to add the new element at the beginning
theArray.unshift(response);

console.log(theArray); // Output: [34, 23, 45, 12, 67]
```

This approach has a time complexity of \(O(1)\), making it more efficient than your original method which has a time complexity of \(O(n)\). The `unshift` method modifies the original array directly.

### Explanation
- **`unshift(element1[, ...[, elementN]])`**: This method adds one or more elements to the beginning of an array and returns the new length of the array.
- By calling `theArray.unshift(response)`, you effectively prepend the `response` element to the beginning of `theArray`.

This method is both concise and efficient, ensuring that your code remains readable and performant.

To empty an array in JavaScript, you can set its length to 0 or use the `splice()` method to remove all elements. Here are two examples of how to do this:

1. Using the `splice()` method:
```javascript
let A = [1, 2, 3, 4];

// Remove all elements from the array
A.splice(0, A.length);

console.log(A); // Output: []
```

2. Setting the length to 0:
```javascript
let A = [1, 2, 3, 4];

// Empty the array
A.length = 0;

console.log(A); // Output: []
```

Both methods effectively empty the array, and the second method is more efficient as it directly sets the length to 0 without needing to call `splice()`.

If you want to remove elements using a function like `remove()`, you can create a custom function that iterates through the array and removes each element one by one. Here's an example:
```javascript
function removeElement(array, element) {
  for (let i = array.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    if (array[i] === element) {
      array.splice(i, 1);
    }
  }
}

let A = [1, 2, 3, 4];

// Remove all elements from the array
removeElement(A, 1);
removeElement(A, 2);
removeElement(A, 3);
removeElement(A, 4);

console.log(A); // Output: []
```

In this example, the `removeElement()` function takes an array and an element as parameters. It iterates through the array in reverse order and removes each occurrence of the specified element using the `splice()` method. Finally, the array is empty.
