To temporarily disable a foreign key constraint in MySQL, you can use the `ALTER TABLE` statement with the `DISABLE KEYS` clause. Here's an example:
<pre><code>ALTER TABLE myapp_item DISABLE KEYS;
DELETE FROM myapp_item WHERE n = %s;
ALTER TABLE myapp_item ENABLE KEYS;
</code></pre>
This will disable the foreign key constraint on the `myapp_item` table, delete the desired records, and then re-enable the constraint.
If you want to disable all foreign key constraints in the database, you can use the `ALTER DATABASE` statement:
<pre><code>ALTER DATABASE mydatabase DISABLE KEYS;
DELETE FROM myapp_item WHERE n = %s;
ALTER DATABASE mydatabase ENABLE KEYS;
</code></pre>
This will disable all foreign key constraints in the `mydatabase` database, delete the desired records, and then re-enable all foreign key constraints in the database.
Note that disabling foreign key constraints can lead to data integrity issues, so use this approach with caution. It's generally better to fix the data issue that caused the foreign key constraint to fail in the first place.

