Title: How do I iterate over the words of a string?.
Body: <p>How do I iterate over the words of a string composed of words separated by whitespace?</p>
<p>Note that I'm not interested in C string functions or that kind of character manipulation/access. I prefer elegance over efficiency. My current solution:</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;
#include &lt;sstream&gt;
#include &lt;string&gt;

using namespace std;

int main() {
    string s = &quot;Somewhere down the road&quot;;
    istringstream iss(s);

    do {
        string subs;
        iss &gt;&gt; subs;
        cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Substring: &quot; &lt;&lt; subs &lt;&lt; endl;
    } while (iss);
}
</code></pre>

Answers:
Here's a Python solution that uses the `split()` method to split the string into a list of words and then iterates over the list using a for loop:
<pre><code>string = &quot;Somewhere down the road&quot;
words = string.split()
for word in words:
    print(word)
</code></pre>
This code splits the string into a list of words using the `split()` method, which splits the string at each occurrence of whitespace by default. It then iterates over the list using a for loop and prints each word. This solution is more elegant and efficient than the C++ solution you provided.

However, if you still want to stick with C++ string manipulation, you can use the `getline()` function from the `<string>` header to read each word from the string one by one. Here's an example:
<pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;
#include &lt;string&gt;

using namespace std;

int main() {
    string s = &quot;Somewhere down the road&quot;;
    istringstream iss(s);

    string word;
    while (getline(iss, word, ' ')) {
        cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Substring: &quot; &lt;&lt; word &lt;&lt; endl;
    }
}
</code></pre>
This code uses the `getline()` function to read each word from the string one by one, using a space as the delimiter. It then prints each word using `cout`. This solution is more efficient than the first solution, but it still requires more code than the Python solution.

If you're interested in a more concise and elegant solution, you can use the `stringstream` class from the `<sstream>` header to read each word from the string one by one using a for loop. Here's an example:
<pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;
#include &lt;sstream&gt;
#include &lt;string&gt;

using namespace std;

int main() {
    string s = &quot;Somewhere down the road&quot;;
    istringstream iss(s);

    for (string word; iss &gt;&gt; word;) {
        cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Substring: &quot; &lt;&lt; word &lt;&lt; endl;
    }
}
</code></pre>
This code uses a for loop to read each word from the string one by one using the `operator&gt;&gt;` function of the `stringstream` class. It then prints each word using `cout`. This solution is more elegant and efficient than the previous solutions, but it still requires more code than the Python solution.

I hope this helps! Let me know if you have any questions or if you need further assistance.
