Title: Android &quot;Only the original thread that created a view hierarchy can touch its views.&quot;.
Body: <p>I've built a simple music player in Android. The view for each song contains a SeekBar, implemented like this: </p>

<pre><code>public class Song extends Activity implements OnClickListener,Runnable {
    private SeekBar progress;
    private MediaPlayer mp;

    // ...

    private ServiceConnection onService = new ServiceConnection() {
          public void onServiceConnected(ComponentName className,
            IBinder rawBinder) {
              appService = ((MPService.LocalBinder)rawBinder).getService(); // service that handles the MediaPlayer
              progress.setVisibility(SeekBar.VISIBLE);
              progress.setProgress(0);
              mp = appService.getMP();
              appService.playSong(title);
              progress.setMax(mp.getDuration());
              new Thread(Song.this).start();
          }
          public void onServiceDisconnected(ComponentName classname) {
              appService = null;
          }
    };

    public void onCreate(Bundle savedInstanceState) {
        super.onCreate(savedInstanceState);
        setContentView(R.layout.song);

        // ...

        progress = (SeekBar) findViewById(R.id.progress);

        // ...
    }

    public void run() {
    int pos = 0;
    int total = mp.getDuration();
    while (mp != null &amp;&amp; pos&lt;total) {
        try {
            Thread.sleep(1000);
            pos = appService.getSongPosition();
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            return;
        } catch (Exception e) {
            return;
        }
        progress.setProgress(pos);
    }
}
</code></pre>

<p>This works fine. Now I want a timer counting the seconds/minutes of the progress of the song. So I put a <code>TextView</code> in the layout, get it with <code>findViewById()</code> in <code>onCreate()</code>, and put this in <code>run()</code> after <code>progress.setProgress(pos)</code>:</p>

<pre><code>String time = String.format("%d:%d",
            TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS.toMinutes(pos),
            TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS.toSeconds(pos),
            TimeUnit.MINUTES.toSeconds(TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS.toMinutes(
                    pos))
            );
currentTime.setText(time);  // currentTime = (TextView) findViewById(R.id.current_time);
</code></pre>

<p>But that last line gives me the exception:</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>android.view.ViewRoot$CalledFromWrongThreadException: Only the original thread that created a view hierarchy can touch its views.</p>
</blockquote>

<p>Yet I'm doing basically the same thing here as I'm doing with the <code>SeekBar</code> - creating the view in <code>onCreate</code>, then touching it in <code>run()</code> - and it doesn't give me this complaint.</p>

Answers:
Certainly! Daemon threads in Java are a type of background thread that runs in the background, without blocking the termination of the application. They are often used for tasks that do not require the program to wait for their completion, such as garbage collection or monitoring. Unlike user threads, daemon threads are not guaranteed to complete before the application exits. To create a daemon thread in Java, you can set the `setDaemon` method to `true` on a `Thread` object before starting it. Here's an example:</p>

```java
Thread daemonThread = new Thread(() -> {
    // Your code here
});
daemonThread.setDaemon(true);
daemonThread.start();
```

This will create a daemon thread that will run in the background and will not prevent the application from exiting. It's important to note that daemon threads should not depend on user threads for their execution, as they may not complete before the application exits.

Certainly! Here are two examples to illustrate the differences between `Service` and `IntentService` in the Android platform:

### Example 1: Using `Service` to Download a File
#### Code:
```java
public class DownloadService extends Service {
    private DownloadManager downloadManager;
    private DownloadManager.Request request;

    @Override
    public void onCreate() {
        super.onCreate();
        downloadManager = (DownloadManager) getSystemService(Context.DOWNLOAD_SERVICE);
        request = new DownloadManager.Request(Uri.parse("http://example.com/file.zip"));
        request.setTitle("File Download");
        request.setDescription("Downloading...");
        request.setNotificationVisibility(DownloadManager.Request.VISIBILITY_VISIBLE_NOTIFY_COMPLETED);
        request.setDestinationInExternalPublicDir(Environment.DIRECTORY_DOWNLOADS, "file.zip");
        downloadManager.enqueue(request);
    }

    @Override
    public IBinder onBind(Intent intent) {
        return null;
    }
}
```

#### Explanation:
- **DownloadService**: This service handles the download process. It uses the `DownloadManager` to enqueue a download request and manages the download lifecycle.
- **onCreate()**: The `onCreate()` method initializes the `DownloadManager` and sets up the download request.
- **onBind()**: Since this is a foreground service, we return `null` as there's no explicit binding mechanism.

### Example 2: Using `IntentService` to Handle Long Running Tasks
#### Code:
```java
public class MyIntentService extends IntentService {
    public MyIntentService() {
        super("MyIntentService");
    }

    @Override
    protected void onHandleIntent(Intent intent) {
        // Perform long-running task here
        Log.d("MyIntentService", "Handling long-running task...");
    }
}
```

#### Explanation:
- **MyIntentService**: This intent service handles long-running tasks in a separate thread. It uses the `onHandleIntent()` method to perform the task.
- **super("MyIntentService")**: Initializes the service with a name.
- **onHandleIntent()**: The method where the actual work is done. It runs in a separate thread managed by the `IntentService`.

### Differences Between `Service` and `IntentService`:
1. **Thread Management**:
   - **Service**: Services run in the main thread by default. You can start a new thread within the service if needed.
   - **IntentService**: Intent services run their tasks in a separate worker thread. This ensures that the main thread remains responsive.

2. **Lifecycle**:
   - **Service**: Can be started, stopped, and restarted multiple times.
   - **IntentService**: Once started, it finishes after completing all tasks. It's designed for short-lived, one-time tasks.

3. **Notification Visibility**:
   - **Service**: Can show notifications.
   - **IntentService**: By default, does not show notifications. To show notifications, you need to use a foreground service.

### Starting a Service in a Separate Thread
You can start a service in a separate thread using `startService()` and `Intent`:
```java
public class MainActivity extends AppCompatActivity {
    private Button startServiceButton;

    @Override
    protected void onCreate(Bundle savedInstanceState) {
        super.onCreate(savedInstanceState);
        setContentView(R.layout.activity_main);

        startServiceButton = findViewById(R.id.startServiceButton);
        startServiceButton.setOnClickListener(v -> {
            Intent serviceIntent = new Intent(this, DownloadService.class);
            startService(serviceIntent);
        });
    }
}
```

This way, you can start the `DownloadService` in a separate thread if needed.

### Conclusion
- **Use `Service`** when you need to handle tasks that are not long-running and require more control over their lifecycle.
- **Use `IntentService`** when you need to perform long-running tasks in a separate thread and want to avoid blocking the main thread.

I hope these examples help clarify the differences between `Service` and `IntentService`! Let me know if you have any further questions.

None. This is a complex question that requires a deep understanding of object-oriented design principles and the specific needs of your application. I'll try to provide a comprehensive answer that covers the key points you raised.

**Why not inherit from `List<T>`?**

There are several reasons why you should avoid inheriting from `List<T>`:

1. **Performance**: As you mentioned, `List<T>` is optimized for performance. Inheriting from it can lead to performance degradation because the derived class needs to override every method of the base class, which can be inefficient.
2. **Inheritance limitations**: Inheritance is a powerful tool, but it also has limitations. When you inherit from a class, you're essentially creating a new type that inherits all the properties, methods, and events of the base class. However, this can lead to issues like:
	* **Conflicting implementations**: If the base class and derived class have methods with the same name, the derived class's implementation might not work as expected.
	* **Overriding restrictions**: You can't override certain methods, like `Finalize`, `Equals`, or `GetHashCode`, which can lead to bugs.
3. **Design principles**: Inheritance is a fundamental concept in object-oriented design, but it's not always the best solution. Design patterns like composition and aggregation are often more suitable for representing relationships between objects.

**Alternative approaches**

Instead of inheriting from `List<T>`, consider these alternatives:

1. **Composition**: Instead of inheriting from `List<T>`, create a new class that contains an instance of `List<T>` as a private field. This allows you to encapsulate the list and provide additional functionality without inheriting from `List<T>`.
2. **Aggregation**: Use aggregation to represent a relationship between objects. For example, a football team could have a collection of players, but the team itself is not a collection.
3. **Collection classes**: Use collection classes like `Collection<T>` or `List<T>` directly, but avoid inheriting from them.

**Example code**

Here's an example of using composition to represent a football team:
```csharp
public class FootballTeam
{
    private List<FootballPlayer> players;

    public FootballTeam()
    {
        this.players = new List<FootballPlayer>();
    }

    public void AddPlayer(FootballPlayer player)
    {
        this.players.Add(player);
    }

    public int Count
    {
        get { return this.players.Count; }
    }
}
```
In this example, the `FootballTeam` class contains a private field of type `List<FootballPlayer>`. The `AddPlayer` method adds a player to the list, and the `Count` property returns the number of players in the team.

**Conclusion**

While it's tempting to inherit from `List<T>` to represent a collection of objects, it's generally not recommended. Instead, consider using composition or aggregation to represent relationships between objects. By avoiding inheritance, you'll create more maintainable and flexible code.

Here's how you can sort a list of <code>Order</code> objects by a property in the object:

<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>public class Order
{
    public int OrderId { get; set; }
    public DateTime OrderDate { get; set; }
    public int Quantity { get; set; }
    public decimal Total { get; set; }
}

public static void SortOrderList(List&lt;Order&gt; objListOrder, string sortProperty)
{
    switch (sortProperty)
    {
        case "OrderDate":
            objListOrder.Sort((a, b) =&gt; a.OrderDate.CompareTo(b.OrderDate));
            break;
        case "OrderId":
            objListOrder.Sort((a, b) =&gt; a.OrderId.CompareTo(b.OrderId));
            break;
        default:
            throw new ArgumentException("Invalid sort property");
    }
}

// Example usage:
List&lt;Order&gt; objListOrder = new List&lt;Order&gt;();
GetOrderList(objListOrder); // fill list of orders
SortOrderList(objListOrder, "OrderDate"); // sort by OrderDate
</code></pre>

This code defines a method <code>SortOrderList</code> that takes a list of <code>Order</code> objects and a string representing the property to sort by. It uses a <code>switch</code> statement to sort the list based on the selected property. If the property is not valid, it throws an <code>ArgumentException</code>.

You can then call this method with the list of <code>Order</code> objects and the property to sort by. In the example above, we sort the list by <code>OrderDate</code>.

This code assumes that the <code>Order</code> class has properties with public getter and setter methods. If your properties are private or have different access modifiers, you'll need to modify the code accordingly. Additionally, if you want to sort by multiple properties, you'll need to use a different approach, such as using a <code>Comparer&lt;Order&gt;</code> class.

Questions:
Title: How do I check if an array includes a value in JavaScript?.
Body: <p>What is the most concise and efficient way to find out if a JavaScript array contains a value?</p>
<p>This is the only way I know to do it:</p>
<pre><code>function contains(a, obj) {
    for (var i = 0; i &lt; a.length; i++) {
        if (a[i] === obj) {
            return true;
        }
    }
    return false;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Is there a better and more concise way to accomplish this?</p>
<p>This is very closely related to Stack Overflow question <em><a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/143847/best-way-to-find-an-item-in-a-javascript-array">Best way to find an item in a JavaScript Array?</a></em> which addresses finding objects in an array using <code>indexOf</code>.</p>

Title: What does O(log n) mean exactly?.
Body: <p>I am learning about Big O Notation running times and amortized times.  I understand the notion of <em>O(n)</em> linear time, meaning that the size of the input affects the growth of the algorithm proportionally...and the same goes for, for example, quadratic time <em>O(n<sup>2</sup>)</em> etc..even algorithms, such as permutation generators, with <em>O(n!)</em> times, that grow by factorials.</p>

<p>For example, the following function is <em>O(n)</em> because the algorithm grows in proportion to its input <em>n</em>:</p>

<pre><code>f(int n) {
  int i;
  for (i = 0; i &lt; n; ++i)
    printf("%d", i);
}
</code></pre>

<p>Similarly, if there was a nested loop, the time would be O(n<sup>2</sup>).</p>

<p>But what exactly is <em>O(log n)</em>?  For example, what does it mean to say that the height of a complete binary tree is <em>O(log n)</em>?</p>

<p>I do know (maybe not in great detail) what Logarithm is, in the sense that:  log<sub>10</sub> 100 = 2, but I cannot understand how to identify a function with a logarithmic time.</p>

Answers:
Tail recursion is a special type of recursion in which the recursive call is the last operation in the function. This means that the function does not perform any additional operations after making the recursive call. In contrast, non-tail recursion involves additional operations after the recursive call. Tail recursion can be optimized by the compiler to avoid stack overflow errors and improve performance, as the compiler can reuse the current stack frame for the next recursive call instead of creating a new one. In Python, you can use the `functools.lru_cache` decorator to implement tail recursion optimization. Here is an example of a tail-recursive function in Python:</p>
```python
from functools import lru_cache

@lru_cache(maxsize=None)
def factorial(n, acc=1):
    if n == 0:
        return acc
    else:
        return factorial(n-1, acc*n)
```
In this example, the `factorial` function is tail-recursive because the recursive call is the last operation in the function. The `acc` parameter is used to accumulate the result of the recursive calls. The `lru_cache` decorator is used to optimize the function by caching the results of previous calls to avoid redundant computations.
