Yes, you can use the <code>getBoundingClientRect()</code> method in JavaScript to get the dimensions of an image on the page. Here is an example code snippet:</p>

<pre><code>var image = document.getElementById('myImage');
var rect = image.getBoundingClientRect();
var width = rect.width;
var height = rect.height;
</code></pre>

<p>Here, <code>myImage</code> is the ID of the image element on the page. The <code>getBoundingClientRect()</code> method returns an object with properties <code>top</code>, <code>right</code>, <code>bottom</code>, <code>left</code>, <code>width</code>, and <code>height</code>, which you can use to get the dimensions of the image. The <code>width</code> and <code>height</code> properties give you the dimensions of the image in pixels.</p>

<p>If you want to get the dimensions of an image without using the <code>getBoundingClientRect()</code> method, you can use the <code>naturalWidth</code> and <code>naturalHeight</code> properties of the image element. Here is an example code snippet:</p>

<pre><code>var image = document.getElementById('myImage');
var width = image.naturalWidth;
var height = image.naturalHeight;
</code></pre>

<p>Here, <code>myImage</code> is the ID of the image element on the page. The <code>naturalWidth</code> and <code>naturalHeight</code> properties give you the dimensions of the image in pixels.</p>

