}
```

And here is the HTML code:

```html
<img src="image.png" class="image" />
```

This will change the color of the image to red. You can change the color to any color you like by changing the value of the color property in the CSS file.

You can also change the color of a PNG image using JavaScript by adding an event listener to the image tag and changing the color in the event handler. Here is an example:

```javascript
var image = document.getElementById("image");
image.addEventListener("click", function() {
    this.style.color = "red";
});
```

And here is the HTML code:

```html
<img id="image" src="image.png" />
```

This will change the color of the image to red when it is clicked. You can change the color to any color you like by changing the value of the color property in the event handler.<|fim_middle|>

Questions:
Title: Is it possible to set a src attribute of an img tag in CSS?.
Body: <p>Is it possible to set the <code>src</code> attribute value in CSS? <br>
In most cases, we use it like this:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;img src=&quot;pathTo/myImage.jpg&quot; /&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>and I want it to be something like this</p>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;img class=&quot;myClass&quot; /&gt;
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>.myClass {
    some-src-property: url(&quot;pathTo/myImage.jpg&quot;);
}
</code></pre>
<p>I want to know if there is a way doing it <strong>without</strong> using the <code>background</code> or <code>background-image</code> properties in CSS.</p>

Answers:
Yes, it is possible to set the <code>src</code> attribute value in CSS, but it is not recommended. The <code>src</code> attribute is used to specify the URL of an image, and it is not a valid CSS property. Instead, you can use the <code>background</code> or <code>background-image</code> properties to set the image as a background for an element.

Here is an example of how you can use the <code>background</code> property to set the image as a background for an element:

<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>.myClass {
    background-image: url("pathTo/myImage.jpg");
}
</code></pre>
However, it is recommended to use the <code>background</code> property instead of the <code>src</code> attribute because it is more flexible and allows you to set the image as a background for an element, whereas the <code>src</code> attribute is only used to specify the URL of an image and cannot be used to set the image as a background.
