Questions:
Title: JavaScript post request like a form submit.
Body: <p>I'm trying to direct a browser to a different page. If I wanted a GET request, I might say</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>document.location.href = 'http://example.com/q=a';
</code></pre>
<p>But the resource I'm trying to access won't respond properly unless I use a POST request. If this were not dynamically generated, I might use the HTML</p>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;form action=&quot;http://example.com/&quot; method=&quot;POST&quot;&gt;
    &lt;input type=&quot;hidden&quot; name=&quot;q&quot; value=&quot;a&quot;&gt;
&lt;/form&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Then I would just submit the form from the DOM.</p>
<p>But really I would like JavaScript code that allows me to say</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>post_to_url('http://example.com/', {'q':'a'});
</code></pre>
<p>What's the best cross browser implementation?</p>
<p>I need a solution that changes the location of the browser, just like submitting a form. If this is possible with <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/XMLHttpRequest" rel="noreferrer">XMLHttpRequest</a>, it is not obvious. And this should not be asynchronous, nor use XML, so Ajax is not the answer.</p>

Answers:
Certainly! To prevent users from submitting a form by hitting Enter, you can use JavaScript to listen for the `keydown` event on the form and check if the pressed key is the Enter key (key code 13). If it is, you can prevent the default form submission behavior. Here's an example implementation using jQuery:

```javascript
$(document).ready(function() {
  // Listen for keydown events on the form
  $('#survey-form').on('keydown', function(event) {
    // Check if the Enter key (key code 13) is pressed
    if (event.keyCode === 13) {
      // Prevent the default form submission behavior
      event.preventDefault();
    }
  });
});
```

In this code, we first wait for the document to be ready using `$(document).ready()`. Then, we attach a `keydown` event listener to the form with the ID `survey-form`. Inside the event handler, we check if the pressed key is the Enter key (key code 13) using `event.keyCode`. If it is, we prevent the default form submission behavior by calling `event.preventDefault()`.

You can add this JavaScript code to your HTML file within a `<script>` tag, or include it in an external JavaScript file.

If you're using jQuery, make sure you have it included in your project. You can include it by adding the following script tag in your HTML file:

```html
<script src="https://code.jquery.com/jquery-3.6.0.min.js"></script>
```

Replace the URL with the appropriate version of jQuery if needed.

This code should prevent users from submitting the form by hitting Enter, ensuring that the submit button is always used to submit the form.

Questions:
Title: Send POST data using XMLHttpRequest.
Body: <p>I'd like to send some data using an XMLHttpRequest in JavaScript.</p>
<p>Say I have the following form in HTML:</p>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;form name=&quot;inputform&quot; action=&quot;somewhere&quot; method=&quot;post&quot;&gt;
  &lt;input type=&quot;hidden&quot; value=&quot;person&quot; name=&quot;user&quot;&gt;
  &lt;input type=&quot;hidden&quot; value=&quot;password&quot; name=&quot;pwd&quot;&gt;
  &lt;input type=&quot;hidden&quot; value=&quot;place&quot; name=&quot;organization&quot;&gt;
  &lt;input type=&quot;hidden&quot; value=&quot;key&quot; name=&quot;requiredkey&quot;&gt;
&lt;/form&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>How can I write the equivalent using an XMLHttpRequest in JavaScript?</p>

Answers:
Here's an example of how you could send the form data using an XMLHttpRequest in JavaScript:
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>var xhr = new XMLHttpRequest();
xhr.open('POST', 'somewhere', true);
xhr.setRequestHeader('Content-Type', 'application/x-www-form-urlencoded');
xhr.send('user=person&pwd=password&organization=place&requiredkey=key');
</code></pre>
This code creates a new XMLHttpRequest object, sets the method and URL, sets the Content-Type header to application/x-www-form-urlencoded, and then sends the form data as a query string in the request body. The data is encoded as a series of key-value pairs separated by ampersands (&amp;).
Note that you'll need to handle the response from the server in the onreadystatechange event handler, or use the onload or onerror event handlers depending on your needs.
Also, make sure to replace 'somewhere' with the actual URL of the server-side script that will handle the request.
This code should work in most modern browsers, but you may need to use a polyfill or a shim to support older browsers that do not natively support XMLHttpRequest.
I hope this helps! Let me know if you have any questions.
<|fim_middle|>

You can disable the resize grabber of a textarea by adding the following CSS code:
```
textarea {
  resize: none;
}
```
This will prevent the user from resizing the textarea by dragging the grabber.
You can also use the `user-select` property to prevent the user from selecting text in the textarea:
```
textarea {
  resize: none;
  user-select: none;
}
```
This will prevent the user from selecting any text in the textarea, including the resize grabber.
You can also use the `overflow` property to prevent the textarea from scrolling when the user resizes it:
```
textarea {
  resize: none;
  user-select: none;
  overflow: hidden;
}
```
This will prevent the textarea from scrolling when the user resizes it, so the resize grabber will be hidden.
You can also use the `pointer-events` property to prevent the resize grabber from being clickable:
```
textarea {
  resize: none;
  user-select: none;
  overflow: hidden;
  pointer-events: none;
}
```
This will prevent the user from being able to click on the resize grabber, so it will not appear as a clickable element.
You can also use JavaScript to disable the resize grabber of a textarea:
```
var textarea = document.querySelector('textarea');
textarea.style.resize = 'none';
```
This will prevent the user from resizing the textarea by dragging the grabber.
You can also use JavaScript to disable the resize grabber of a textarea and prevent the user from selecting any text in it:
```
var textarea = document.querySelector('textarea');
textarea.style.resize = 'none';
textarea.style.userSelect = 'none';
```
This will prevent the user from resizing the textarea by dragging the grabber and prevent the user from selecting any text in it.
You can also use JavaScript to disable the resize grabber of a textarea and prevent the user from selecting any text in it and hide the resize grabber:
```
var textarea = document.querySelector('textarea');
textarea.style.resize = 'none';
textarea.style.userSelect = 'none';
textarea.style.overflow = 'hidden';
```
This will prevent the user from resizing the textarea by dragging the grabber, prevent the user from selecting any text in it, and hide the resize grabber.
You can also use JavaScript to disable the resize grabber of a textarea and prevent the user from selecting any text in it and hide the resize grabber and prevent the user from clicking on the resize grabber:
```
var textarea = document.querySelector('textarea');
textarea.style.resize = 'none';
textarea.style.userSelect = 'none';
textarea.style.overflow = 'hidden';
textarea.style.pointerEvents = 'none';
```
This will prevent the user from resizing the textarea by dragging the grabber, prevent the user from selecting any text in it, hide the resize grabber, and prevent the user from clicking on the resize grabber.

Questions:
Title: How can I get form data with JavaScript/jQuery?.
Body: <p>Is there a simple, one-line way to get the data of a form as it would be if it was to be submitted in the classic HTML-only way?</p>

<p>For example:</p>

<pre><code>&lt;form&gt;
    &lt;input type="radio" name="foo" value="1" checked="checked" /&gt;
    &lt;input type="radio" name="foo" value="0" /&gt;
    &lt;input name="bar" value="xxx" /&gt;
    &lt;select name="this"&gt;
        &lt;option value="hi" selected="selected"&gt;Hi&lt;/option&gt;
        &lt;option value="ho"&gt;Ho&lt;/option&gt;
&lt;/form&gt;
</code></pre>

<p>Output:</p>

<pre><code>{
    "foo": "1",
    "bar": "xxx",
    "this": "hi"
}
</code></pre>

<p>Something like this is too simple, since it does not (correctly) include textareas, selects, radio buttons and checkboxes:</p>

<pre><code>$("#form input").each(function () {
    data[theFieldName] = theFieldValue;
});
</code></pre>

Title: var functionName = function() {} vs function functionName() {}.
Body: <p>I've recently started maintaining someone else's JavaScript code. I'm fixing bugs, adding features and also trying to tidy up the code and make it more consistent.</p>
<p>The previous developer used two ways of declaring functions and I can't work out if there is a reason behind it or not.</p>
<p>The two ways are:</p>
<pre><code>var functionOne = function() {
    // Some code
};
</code></pre>
<p>And,</p>
<pre><code>function functionTwo() {
    // Some code
}
</code></pre>
<p>What are the reasons for using these two different methods and what are the pros and cons of each? Is there anything that can be done with one method that can't be done with the other?</p>

Title: What is the difference between call and apply?.
Body: <p>What is the difference between using <code>Function.prototype.apply()</code> and <code>Function.prototype.call()</code> to invoke a function?</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const func = function() {
    alert(&quot;Hello world!&quot;);
};
</code></pre>
<p><code>func.apply()</code> vs. <code>func.call()</code></p>
<p>Are there performance differences between the two aforementioned methods? When is it best to use <code>call</code> over <code>apply</code> and vice versa?</p>

Answers:
Here's the answer to your question:

The main difference between <code>Function.prototype.apply()</code> and <code>Function.prototype.call()</code> is how they handle arguments. <code>apply()</code> takes an array of arguments, while <code>call()</code> takes individual arguments.

Here's an example to illustrate the difference:

<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const func = function(a, b) {
    alert(a + &quot; &quot; + b);
};

func.call(null, &quot;Hello&quot;, &quot;world!&quot;); // &quot;Hello world!&quot;
func.apply(null, [&quot;Hello&quot;, &quot;world!&quot;]); // &quot;Hello world!&quot;
</code></pre>

In this example, both <code>func.call()</code> and <code>func.apply()</code> are used to invoke the <code>func</code> function with two arguments: &quot;Hello&quot; and &quot;world!&quot;. The output of both calls is the same: &quot;Hello world!&quot;.

However, if you want to pass an array of arguments to <code>apply()</code>, you can do so like this:

<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const args = [&quot;Hello&quot;, &quot;world!&quot;];
func.apply(null, args); // &quot;Hello world!&quot;
</code></pre>

In this example, <code>args</code> is an array containing the two arguments to pass to the <code>func</code> function. <code>apply()</code> is then used to invoke the <code>func</code> function with the arguments from the <code>args</code> array.

As for performance, there is no significant difference between the two methods. Both <code>apply()</code> and <code>call()</code> are implemented in JavaScript engines and are optimized for performance.

In general, <code>call()</code> is recommended for invoking functions with individual arguments, while <code>apply()</code> is recommended for invoking functions with an array of arguments. However, this is just a guideline, and the choice between the two methods ultimately depends on the specific use case.

Use <code>call()</code> when you want to explicitly pass individual arguments to a function. Use <code>apply()</code> when you want to pass an array of arguments to a function. Both methods will achieve the same result, but one may be more appropriate depending on your specific use case.

