Title: Loop through an array in JavaScript.
Body: <p>In Java, you can use a <code>for</code> loop to traverse objects in an array as follows:</p>
<pre><code>String[] myStringArray = {&quot;Hello&quot;, &quot;World&quot;};
for (String s : myStringArray) {
    // Do something
}
</code></pre>
<p>Can I do the same in JavaScript?</p>

Answers:
Yes, you can use a <code>for</code> loop in JavaScript to traverse objects in an array. Here is an example:
<pre><code>let myStringArray = [&quot;Hello&quot;, &quot;World&quot;];
for (let i = 0; i &lt; myStringArray.length; i++) {
    console.log(myStringArray[i]);
}
</code></pre>
You can also use a <code>for..of</code> loop to traverse objects in an array in JavaScript. Here is an example:
<pre><code>let myStringArray = [&quot;Hello&quot;, &quot;World&quot;];
for (let s of myStringArray) {
    console.log(s);
}
</code></pre>
Both loops will iterate over the elements of the array and print them to the console. The <code>for..of</code> loop is generally considered more readable and is the recommended way to traverse arrays in JavaScript.
<|fim_middle|>

