Title: JavaScript closure inside loops – simple practical example.
Body: <p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>var funcs = [];
// let's create 3 functions
for (var i = 0; i &lt; 3; i++) {
  // and store them in funcs
  funcs[i] = function() {
    // each should log its value.
    console.log("My value:", i);
  };
}
for (var j = 0; j &lt; 3; j++) {
  // and now let's run each one to see
  funcs[j]();
}</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>It outputs this:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>My value: 3<br />
My value: 3<br />
My value: 3</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Whereas I'd like it to output:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>My value: 0<br />
My value: 1<br />
My value: 2</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<p>The same problem occurs when the delay in running the function is caused by using event listeners:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>var buttons = document.getElementsByTagName("button");
// let's create 3 functions
for (var i = 0; i &lt; buttons.length; i++) {
  // as event listeners
  buttons[i].addEventListener("click", function() {
    // each should log its value.
    console.log("My value:", i);
  });
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;button&gt;0&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;button&gt;1&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;button&gt;2&lt;/button&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>… or asynchronous code, e.g. using Promises:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>// Some async wait function
const wait = (ms) =&gt; new Promise((resolve, reject) =&gt; setTimeout(resolve, ms));

for (var i = 0; i &lt; 3; i++) {
  // Log `i` as soon as each promise resolves.
  wait(i * 100).then(() =&gt; console.log(i));
}</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>It is also apparent in <code>for in</code> and <code>for of</code> loops:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const arr = [1,2,3];
const fns = [];

for (var i in arr){
  fns.push(() =&gt; console.log("index:", i));
}

for (var v of arr){
  fns.push(() =&gt; console.log("value:", v));
}

for (const n of arr) {
  var obj = { number: n }; // or new MyLibObject({ ... })
  fns.push(() =&gt; console.log("n:", n, "|", "obj:", JSON.stringify(obj)));
}

for(var f of fns){
  f();
}</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>What’s the solution to this basic problem?</p>

Answers:
Certainly! The issue you're facing is due to JavaScript closures capturing the variable `i` at the time the loop is executed, rather than when each function is called. To solve this, you can use one of the following approaches:

1. **Use a closure with an immediately invoked function expression (IIFE)**:
   ```javascript
   var funcs = [];
   for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
     (function(i) {
       funcs[i] = function() {
         console.log("My value:", i);
       };
     })(i);
   }
   for (var j = 0; j < 3; j++) {
     funcs[j]();
   }
   ```

2. **Use let instead of var for block-scoped variables**:
   ```javascript
   var funcs = [];
   for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
     funcs[i] = function() {
       console.log("My value:", i);
     };
   }
   for (let j = 0; j < 3; j++) {
     funcs[j]();
   }
   ```

3. **Use an arrow function**:
   ```javascript
   var funcs = [];
   for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
     funcs[i] = () => console.log("My value:", i);
   }
   for (var j = 0; j < 3; j++) {
     funcs[j]();
   }
   ```

4. **Use the `forEach` method**:
   ```javascript
   var funcs = [];
   [0, 1, 2].forEach((i) => {
     funcs.push(() => console.log("My value:", i));
   });
   funcs.forEach((func) => func());
   ```

5. **Use the `map` method**:
   ```javascript
   var funcs = [0, 1, 2].map((i) => () => console.log("My value:", i));
   funcs.forEach((func) => func());
   ```

6. **Use the `bind` method**:
   ```javascript
   var funcs = [];
   for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
     funcs[i] = function() {
       console.log("My value:", i);
     }.bind(null, i);
   }
   for (var j = 0; j < 3; j++) {
     funcs[j]();
   }
   ```

Each of these solutions ensures that the correct value of `i` is captured in the closure, avoiding the issue with the outer `i` variable being used instead.

Questions:
Title: Why is printing &quot;B&quot; dramatically slower than printing &quot;#&quot;?.
Body: <p>I generated two matrices of <code>1000</code> x <code>1000</code>:</p>
<p>First Matrix: <code>O</code> and <code>#</code>.<br />
Second Matrix: <code>O</code> and <code>B</code>.</p>
<p>Using the following code, the first matrix took 8.52 seconds to complete:</p>
<pre><code>Random r = new Random();
for (int i = 0; i &lt; 1000; i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j &lt; 1000; j++) {
        if (r.nextInt(4) == 0) {
            System.out.print(&quot;O&quot;);
        } else {
            System.out.print(&quot;#&quot;);
        }
    }

   System.out.println(&quot;&quot;);
 }
</code></pre>
<p>With this code, the second matrix took 259.152 seconds to complete:</p>
<pre><code>Random r = new Random();
for (int i = 0; i &lt; 1000; i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j &lt; 1000; j++) {
        if (r.nextInt(4) == 0) {
            System.out.print(&quot;O&quot;);
        } else {
            System.out.print(&quot;B&quot;); // only line changed
        }
    }

    System.out.println(&quot;&quot;);
}
</code></pre>
<p>What is the reason behind the dramatically different run times?</p>
<hr />
<p>As suggested in the comments, printing only <code>System.out.print(&quot;#&quot;);</code> takes <code>7.8871</code> seconds, whereas <code>System.out.print(&quot;B&quot;);</code> gives <code>still printing...</code>.</p>
<p>As others pointed out that it works for them normally, I tried <a href="http://ideone.com" rel="noreferrer">Ideone.com</a> for instance, and both pieces of code execute at the same speed.</p>
<p>Test Conditions:</p>
<ul>
<li>I ran this test from <em>Netbeans 7.2</em>, with the output into its console</li>
<li>I used <code>System.nanoTime()</code> for measurements</li>
</ul>

Title: Iterate through object properties.
Body: <p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>var obj = {
    name: "Simon",
    age: "20",
    clothing: {
        style: "simple",
        hipster: false
    }
}

for(var propt in obj){
    console.log(propt + ': ' + obj[propt]);
}</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>

<p>How does the variable <code>propt</code> represent the properties of the object? It's not a built-in method or property. Why does it come up with every property in the object?</p>

Answers:
None

The variable <code>propt</code> represents the properties of the object by iterating over the object's properties using a for...in loop. The loop iterates over each property in the object and assigns the property name to the variable <code>propt</code>. This allows you to access the property name and its corresponding value in the object.

The reason why <code>propt</code> comes up with every property in the object is because the for...in loop iterates over each property in the object, including nested properties. In this case, the object <code>obj</code> has three properties: <code>name</code>, <code>age</code>, and <code>clothing</code>. The for...in loop iterates over these properties and assigns the property name to the variable <code>propt</code>, which is then used to access the corresponding property value in the object.
