Title: How do I break out of nested loops in Java?.
Body: <p>I've got a nested loop construct like this:</p>
<pre><code>for (Type type : types) {
    for (Type t : types2) {
         if (some condition) {
             // Do something and break...
             break; // Breaks out of the inner loop
         }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Now how can I break out of both loops? I've looked at similar questions, but none concerns Java specifically. I couldn't apply these solutions because most used gotos.</p>
<p>I don't want to put the inner loop in a different method.</p>
<p>I don't want to return the loops. When breaking I'm finished with the execution of the loop block.</p>

Title: How to loop through a plain JavaScript object with the objects as members.
Body: <p>How can I loop through all members in a JavaScript object, including values that are objects?</p>
<p>For example, how could I loop through this (accessing the &quot;your_name&quot; and &quot;your_message&quot; for each)?</p>
<pre><code>var validation_messages = {
    &quot;key_1&quot;: {
        &quot;your_name&quot;: &quot;jimmy&quot;,
        &quot;your_msg&quot;: &quot;hello world&quot;
    },
    &quot;key_2&quot;: {
        &quot;your_name&quot;: &quot;billy&quot;,
        &quot;your_msg&quot;: &quot;foo equals bar&quot;
    }
}
</code></pre>

Answers:
Here is a for-loop in Java that iterates through each of the enum values in your <code>Direction</code> enum:
<pre><code>for (Direction direction : Direction.values()) {
    // do something with each direction
}
</code></pre>
This loop will iterate through each of the enum values in <code>Direction</code> and assign them to the variable <code>direction</code> in each iteration. You can then use <code>direction</code> to access the properties and methods of the current enum value in the loop body.
If you want to iterate through the enum values in a specific order, you can use the <code>ordinal()</code> method to get the index of each enum value, and then use a for-loop to iterate through the indices. For example, to iterate through the enum values in the order they were declared in the enum definition, you can use the following code:
<pre><code>for (int i = 0; i < Direction.values().length; i++) {
    Direction direction = Direction.values()[i];
    // do something with each direction
}
</code></pre>
This loop will iterate through each of the enum values in the order they were declared in the enum definition, and assign them to the variable <code>direction</code> in each iteration. You can then use <code>direction</code> to access the properties and methods of the current enum value in the loop body.

Questions:
Title: C# loop - break vs. continue.
Body: <p>In a C# (feel free to answer for other languages) loop, what's the difference between <code>break</code> and <code>continue</code> as a means to leave the structure of the loop, and go to the next iteration?</p>
<p>Example:</p>
<pre><code>foreach (DataRow row in myTable.Rows)
{
    if (someConditionEvalsToTrue)
    {
        break; //what's the difference between this and continue ?
        //continue;
    }
}
</code></pre>

Answers:
In C# (and most other programming languages), the <code>break</code> statement is used to exit the loop entirely, while the <code>continue</code> statement is used to skip the current iteration and move on to the next iteration of the loop.

Here's an example of how to use these statements in a C# loop:

<pre><code>foreach (DataRow row in myTable.Rows)
{
    if (someConditionEvalsToTrue)
    {
        break; // this will exit the loop entirely, and move on to the next line of code
        //continue;
    }
}
</code></pre>

In this example, if <code>someConditionEvalsToTrue</code> is true, the loop will exit entirely and move on to the next line of code. If you want to skip the current iteration and move on to the next iteration, you can use the <code>continue</code> statement:

<pre><code>foreach (DataRow row in myTable.Rows)
{
    if (someConditionEvalsToTrue)
    {
        continue; // this will skip the current iteration and move on to the next iteration of the loop
        //break;
    }
}
</code></pre>

In this example, if <code>someConditionEvalsToTrue</code> is true, the loop will skip the current iteration and move on to the next iteration of the loop.<|fim_middle|>

Questions:
Title: How to set the Content-Type header for an HttpClient request?.
Body: <p>I'm trying to set the <code>Content-Type</code> header of an <code>HttpClient</code> object as required by an API I am calling.</p>

<p>I tried setting the <code>Content-Type</code> like below:</p>

<pre><code>using (var httpClient = new HttpClient())
{
    httpClient.BaseAddress = new Uri("http://example.com/");
    httpClient.DefaultRequestHeaders.Add("Accept", "application/json");
    httpClient.DefaultRequestHeaders.Add("Content-Type", "application/json");
    // ...
}
</code></pre>

<p>It allows me to add the <code>Accept</code> header but when I try to add <code>Content-Type</code> it throws the following exception:</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>Misused header name. Make sure request headers are used with
  <code>HttpRequestMessage</code>, response headers with <code>HttpResponseMessage</code>, and
  content headers with <code>HttpContent</code> objects.</p>
</blockquote>

<p>How can I set the <code>Content-Type</code> header in a <code>HttpClient</code> request?</p>

Answers:
To set the <code>Content-Type</code> header in an <code>HttpClient</code> request, you can use the <code>Content</code> property of the <code>HttpRequestMessage</code> object. Here is an example of how to do it:</p>

<pre><code>using (var httpClient = new HttpClient())
{
    httpClient.BaseAddress = new Uri("http://example.com/");
    var request = new HttpRequestMessage(HttpMethod.Post, "api/resource");
    request.Content = new StringContent("{\"key\": \"value\"}", Encoding.UTF8, "application/json");
    // ...
}
</code></pre>

<p>In this example, we create an <code>HttpRequestMessage</code> object with the desired <code>Content-Type</code> and add it to the request using the <code>Content</code> property.</p>

<p>Make sure to replace the <code>\"key\": \"value\"</code> with the actual data you want to send in the request.</p>

<p>This should set the <code>Content-Type</code> header correctly in your <code>HttpClient</code> request.</p>
