Title: What is the difference between &#39;it&#39; and &#39;test&#39; in Jest?.
Body: <p>I have two tests in my test group. One of the tests use <code>it</code> and the other one uses <code>test</code>. Both of them seem to be working very similarly. What is the difference between them?</p>
<pre><code>describe('updateAll', () =&gt; {
  it('no force', () =&gt; {
    return updateAll(TableName, [&quot;fileName&quot;], {compandId: &quot;test&quot;})
        .then(updatedItems =&gt; {
          let undefinedCount = 0;
          for (let item of updatedItems) {
            undefinedCount += item === undefined ? 1 : 0;
          }
          // console.log(&quot;result&quot;, result);
          expect(undefinedCount).toBe(updatedItems.length);
        })
  });

  test('force update', () =&gt; {
    return updateAll(TableName, [&quot;fileName&quot;], {compandId: &quot;test&quot;}, true)
        .then(updatedItems =&gt; {
          let undefinedCount = 0;
          for (let item of updatedItems) {
            undefinedCount += item === undefined ? 1 : 0;
          }
          // console.log(&quot;result&quot;, result);
          expect(undefinedCount).toBe(0);
        })
  });
});
</code></pre>
<hr/>
<p><strong>Update - Nov 2022:</strong></p>
<p>It seems that <code>test</code> and <code>it</code> is interchangeable according to <a href="https://facebook.github.io/jest/docs/api.html" rel="noreferrer">the official API of Jest</a>. As @gwildu described <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/56072272/4056509">here</a>, you should choose one over the other for the sake of <strong>readability</strong>.</p>

Answers:
Certainly! The `internal` access modifier in C# is used to restrict the accessibility of a member to the current assembly only. This means that members marked as `internal` are visible only within the same assembly, but not to other assemblies. This can be very useful when you want to expose certain members to your own testing framework without making them public.

Here are some reasons why you might want to use `internal`:

1. **Testing**: As you mentioned, `internal` members are only visible within the same assembly, so you can use them in your testing projects without exposing them to other parts of your codebase.

2. **Encapsulation**: Using `internal` can help you encapsulate your implementation details within the assembly. This can make your code more flexible and easier to maintain.

3. **Dependency Management**: If you have multiple projects within your solution, using `internal` can help manage dependencies more effectively. For example, you might have a library project that uses `internal` members in another project, and then the library project can be used by other projects without exposing those members directly.

Now, let's discuss when to use `private`:

1. **Private Members**: Private members are accessible only within the class they are defined in. They are not visible to any other classes or namespaces. This is useful for encapsulating implementation details and ensuring that the class's behavior is only controlled through its public interface.

2. **Encapsulation**: Private members help enforce encapsulation by hiding the internal state and implementation details of a class. This can make your code more robust and easier to understand.

3. **Testing**: Sometimes, you might need to access private members for testing purposes. In such cases, you can use reflection to access private members, but this is generally discouraged because it can make your code less maintainable and harder to understand.

To summarize:
- Use `internal` when you want to expose members to your own testing framework within the same assembly.
- Use `private` when you want to hide implementation details and enforce encapsulation within a class.

Here's an example of how you might use `internal` in a testing scenario:

```csharp
public class MyClass
{
    internal void MyInternalMethod()
    {
        // Implementation details
    }
}

// In your testing project
[Test]
public void TestMyClass()
{
    var myClass = new MyClass();
    myClass.MyInternalMethod(); // This will compile and run fine
}
```

And here's an example of how you might use `private`:

```csharp
public class MyClass
{
    private void MyPrivateMethod()
    {
        // Implementation details
    }
}

// In your testing project
[Test]
public void TestMyClass()
{
    var myClass = new MyClass();
    // myClass.MyPrivateMethod(); // This will not compile
}
```

