Title: Understanding Python super() with __init__() methods.
Body: <p>Why is <code>super()</code> used?</p>
<p>Is there a difference between using <code>Base.__init__</code> and <code>super().__init__</code>?</p>
<pre><code>class Base(object):
    def __init__(self):
        print &quot;Base created&quot;

class ChildA(Base):
    def __init__(self):
        Base.__init__(self)

class ChildB(Base):
    def __init__(self):
        super(ChildB, self).__init__()

ChildA() 
ChildB()
</code></pre>

Answers:
To answer this question, let's first understand the difference between <code>struct</code> and <code>class</code> in C++:

<p>A <code>struct</code> is a user-defined data type that allows you to group related data together. It is a lightweight class that does not have any member functions. <code>struct</code>s are typically used when you want to define a simple data structure that does not require any behavior.</p>

<p>A <code>class</code>, on the other hand, is a user-defined data type that allows you to define both data and behavior. It is a more powerful data type than a <code>struct</code>, as it allows you to define member functions that can manipulate the data. <code>class</code>s are typically used when you want to define a more complex data structure that requires behavior.</p>

<p>Here are some scenarios where you might want to use a <code>struct</code> or a <code>class</code> in C++:</p>

<p>Use a <code>struct</code> when:</p>

<ul>
<li>You want to define a simple data structure that does not require any behavior.</li>
<li>You want to define a data structure that will be used as a member of another data structure.</li>
<li>You want to define a data structure that will be used as a return value of a function.</li>
</ul>

<p>Use a <code>class</code> when:</p>

<ul>
<li>You want to define a more complex data structure that requires behavior.</li>
<li>You want to define a data structure that will be used as an argument to a function.</li>
<li>You want to define a data structure that will be used as a member of another data structure.</li>
</ul>

<p>Here is an example of how you might use a <code>struct</code> and a <code>class</code> in C++:</p>

<pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;
#include &lt;vector&gt;

struct Point {
    int x;
    int y;
};

class Circle {
public:
    Circle(int radius) : radius(radius) {}
    int getRadius() const { return radius; }
    void setRadius(int radius) { this-&gt;radius = radius; }
private:
    int radius;
};

int main() {
    Point p1 = {1, 2};
    std::vector&lt;Point&gt; points = {{3, 4}, {5, 6}};
    Circle c1(7);

    std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Point p1: (&quot; &lt;&lt; p1.x &lt;&lt; &quot;, &quot; &lt;&lt; p1.y &lt;&lt; &quot;)&quot; &lt;&lt; std::endl;
    std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Circle c1 radius: &quot; &lt;&lt; c1.getRadius() &lt;&lt; std::endl;

    c1.setRadius(11);
    std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Circle c1 radius after set: &quot; &lt;&lt; c1.getRadius() &lt;&lt; std::endl;

    return 0;
}
</code></pre>

In this example, we define a <code>struct</code> called <code>Point</code> that represents a point in 2D space. We also define a <code>class</code> called <code>Circle</code> that represents a circle in 2D space. We use the <code>struct</code> to define a simple data structure that does not require any behavior, while we use the <code>class</code> to define a more complex data structure that requires behavior.</p>

<p>Overall, the choice between a <code>struct</code> and a <code>class</code> in C++ depends on the specific requirements of your program. If you need a simple data structure that does not require any behavior, then a <code>struct</code> might be the better choice. If you need a more complex data structure that requires behavior, then a <code>class</code> might be the better choice.</p>

