Body: <p>In C#, a static class and a singleton class are both used to create objects that can be accessed without instantiating them. However, there are some key differences between the two:</p>

<h3>Static Classes</h3>

<ul>
  <li>A static class cannot be instantiated. It is a collection of static members that can be accessed directly from the class name.</li>
  <li>Static classes are useful for organizing related functions or constants that do not require state or instance-specific behavior.</li>
  <li>Static classes can contain only static members, such as methods, properties, and fields.</li>
  <li>Static classes cannot be inherited.</li>
  <li>Static classes are sealed, meaning they cannot be inherited by other classes.</li>
  <li>Static classes are static members, meaning they are shared across all instances of the class.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Singleton Classes</h3>

<ul>
  <li>A singleton class is a class that can only have one instance. It is a useful pattern for managing resources or providing a global point of access to a particular object.</li>
  <li>Singleton classes can be instantiated, but only one instance of the class can exist at a time.</li>
  <li>Singleton classes can contain both static and instance members.</li>
  <li>Singleton classes are not sealed, meaning they can be inherited by other classes.</li>
  <li>Singleton classes are typically implemented using a private constructor and a static method that returns the single instance of the class.</li>
</ul>

<p>Here's an example of a static class and a singleton class in C#:</p>

<pre><code>// Static class
public static class MathUtils
{
    public static int Add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }
    public static int Multiply(int a, int b) { return a * b; }
}

// Singleton class
public sealed class Singleton
{
    private static Singleton instance = null;
    private Singleton() { }

    public static Singleton Instance
    {
        get
        {
            if (instance == null)
            {
                instance = new Singleton();
            }
            return instance;
        }
    }

    public void DoSomething()
    {
        // ...
    }
}
</code></pre>

In summary, static classes are useful for organizing related functions or constants that do not require state or instance-specific behavior, while singleton classes are useful for managing resources or providing a global point of access to a particular object.

