Title: Can we instantiate an abstract class?.
Body: <p>During one of my interview, I was asked "If we can instantiate an abstract class?"</p>

<p>My reply was "No. we can't". But, interviewer told me "Wrong, we can." </p>

<p>I argued a bit on this. Then he told me to try this myself at home. </p>

<pre><code>abstract class my {
    public void mymethod() {
        System.out.print("Abstract");
    }
}

class poly {
    public static void main(String a[]) {
        my m = new my() {};
        m.mymethod();
    }
}
</code></pre>

<p>Here, I'm creating instance of my class and calling method of abstract class. Can anyone please explain this to me? Was I really wrong during my interview?</p>

Answers:
Yes, you were right during your interview. Abstract classes cannot be instantiated directly. However, you can create an instance of a subclass that implements the abstract class's methods. In your example, you are trying to create an instance of an abstract class directly, which is not allowed.

<pre><code>abstract class my {
    public void mymethod() {
        System.out.print("Abstract");
    }
}

class poly {
    public static void main(String a[]) {
        my m = new my() {}; // This is not allowed
        m.mymethod();
    }
}
</code></pre>

Instead, you should create an instance of a subclass that implements the abstract class's methods:

<pre><code>abstract class my {
    public abstract void mymethod();
}

class poly extends my {
    public void mymethod() {
        System.out.print("Abstract");
    }
}

class polyMain {
    public static void main(String a[]) {
        my m = new poly();
        m.mymethod();
    }
}
</code></pre>

This way, you can create an instance of the abstract class's subclass and call the abstract class's method. Note that the subclass must implement the abstract class's methods.

I hope this explanation helps! Let me know if you have any more questions.
