Title: Sort ArrayList of custom Objects by property.
Body: <p>I read about sorting ArrayLists using a Comparator but in all of the examples people used <code>compareTo</code> which according to some research is a method for Strings.</p>

<p>I wanted to sort an ArrayList of custom objects by one of their properties: a Date object
(<code>getStartDay()</code>). Normally I compare them by <code>item1.getStartDate().before(item2.getStartDate())</code> so I was wondering whether I could write something like:</p>

<pre><code>public class CustomComparator {
    public boolean compare(Object object1, Object object2) {
        return object1.getStartDate().before(object2.getStartDate());
    }
}

public class RandomName {
    ...
    Collections.sort(Database.arrayList, new CustomComparator);
    ...
}
</code></pre>

Answers:
Certainly! To sort an ArrayList of custom objects by one of their properties (e.g., a `Date` object) using a custom comparator, you can create a comparator class that implements the `Comparator` interface or extends `AbstractComparator`. Here's how you can do it:

1. **Implement the Comparator Interface**: Create a class that implements the `Comparator` interface and override the `compare` method to compare the dates.

```java
import java.util.Comparator;
import java.util.Date;

public class CustomComparator implements Comparator<Object> {
    @Override
    public int compare(Object object1, Object object2) {
        Date startDate1 = ((YourCustomObject) object1).getStartDate();
        Date startDate2 = ((YourCustomObject) object2).getStartDate();

        return startDate1.compareTo(startDate2);
    }
}
```

2. **Sort the ArrayList**: Use the `Collections.sort` method with your custom comparator.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.Date;

public class RandomName {
    private ArrayList<YourCustomObject> arrayList;

    public RandomName() {
        arrayList = new ArrayList<>();
        // Add your custom objects to the list
    }

    public void sortArrayList() {
        Collections.sort(arrayList, new CustomComparator());
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        RandomName randomName = new RandomName();
        randomName.sortArrayList();
        // Now your arrayList is sorted by start date
    }
}
```

3. **Ensure Proper Casting**: Make sure that the objects in your `ArrayList` are instances of your custom class (`YourCustomObject`).

Here's a complete example with a custom object:

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.Date;

public class YourCustomObject {
    private Date startDate;

    public YourCustomObject(Date startDate) {
        this.startDate = startDate;
    }

    public Date getStartDate() {
        return startDate;
    }
}

public class CustomComparator implements Comparator<YourCustomObject> {
    @Override
    public int compare(YourCustomObject object1, YourCustomObject object2) {
        return object1.getStartDate().compareTo(object2.getStartDate());
    }
}

public class RandomName {
    private ArrayList<YourCustomObject> arrayList;

    public RandomName() {
        arrayList = new ArrayList<>();
        arrayList.add(new YourCustomObject(new Date(2023, 1, 1)));
        arrayList.add(new YourCustomObject(new Date(2023, 2, 1)));
        arrayList.add(new YourCustomObject(new Date(2023, 3, 1)));
    }

    public void sortArrayList() {
        Collections.sort(arrayList, new CustomComparator());
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        RandomName randomName = new RandomName();
        randomName.sortArrayList();
        // Now your arrayList is sorted by start date
        for (YourCustomObject obj : randomName.arrayList) {
            System.out.println(obj.getStartDate());
        }
    }
}
```

This example demonstrates how to sort an `ArrayList` of custom objects by a specific property (in this case, a `Date` object) using a custom comparator.

