Title: Why is subtracting these two epoch-milli Times (in year 1927) giving a strange result?.
Body: <p>If I run the following program, which parses two date strings referencing times 1 second apart and compares them:</p>
<pre><code>public static void main(String[] args) throws ParseException {
    SimpleDateFormat sf = new SimpleDateFormat(&quot;yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss&quot;);  
    String str3 = &quot;1927-12-31 23:54:07&quot;;  
    String str4 = &quot;1927-12-31 23:54:08&quot;;  
    Date sDt3 = sf.parse(str3);  
    Date sDt4 = sf.parse(str4);  
    long ld3 = sDt3.getTime() /1000;  
    long ld4 = sDt4.getTime() /1000;
    System.out.println(ld4-ld3);
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>The output is:</strong></p>
<pre><code>353
</code></pre>
<p>Why is <code>ld4-ld3</code>, not <code>1</code> (as I would expect from the one-second difference in the times), but <code>353</code>?</p>
<p>If I change the dates to times 1 second later:</p>
<pre><code>String str3 = &quot;1927-12-31 23:54:08&quot;;  
String str4 = &quot;1927-12-31 23:54:09&quot;;  
</code></pre>
<p>Then <code>ld4-ld3</code> will be <code>1</code>.</p>
<hr />
<p><strong>Java version:</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>java version &quot;1.6.0_22&quot;
Java(TM) SE Runtime Environment (build 1.6.0_22-b04)
Dynamic Code Evolution Client VM (build 0.2-b02-internal, 19.0-b04-internal, mixed mode)
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>Timezone(`TimeZone.getDefault()`):

sun.util.calendar.ZoneInfo[id=&quot;Asia/Shanghai&quot;,
offset=28800000,dstSavings=0,
useDaylight=false,
transitions=19,
lastRule=null]

Locale(Locale.getDefault()): zh_CN
</code></pre>

Answers:
To understand why the subtraction of these two epoch-milli times gives a strange result, let's break down the code and analyze the date parsing and time conversion process.
<p>First, we parse the two date strings into <code>Date</code> objects using the <code>SimpleDateFormat</code> class:</p>
<pre><code>Date sDt3 = sf.parse(str3);  
Date sDt4 = sf.parse(str4);
</code></pre>
<p>Then, we convert the <code>Date</code> objects to epoch-milli times by calling the <code>getTime()</code> method and dividing the result by 1000:</p>
<pre><code>long ld3 = sDt3.getTime() /1000;  
long ld4 = sDt4.getTime() /1000;
</code></pre>
<p>Now, let's examine the two date strings:</p>
<pre><code>String str3 = &quot;1927-12-31 23:54:07&quot;;  
String str4 = &quot;1927-12-31 23:54:08&quot;;  
</code></pre>
<p>Both date strings have the same date (1927-12-31), but different times (23:54:07 and 23:54:08). When we parse these strings into <code>Date</code> objects, the <code>SimpleDateFormat</code> class interprets the time as a 24-hour time (23:54:07 and 23:54:08).</p>
<p>However, when we convert these <code>Date</code> objects to epoch-milli times by calling the <code>getTime()</code> method, the resulting times are not the same. The <code>getTime()</code> method returns the number of milliseconds since the Unix epoch (January 1, 1970, 00:00:00 UTC). In this case, the difference between the two times is 1 second, but the difference in epoch-milli times is 353 milliseconds.</p>
<p>This is because the <code>SimpleDateFormat</code> class uses the default time zone (which is the system's default time zone) when parsing the date strings. In this case, the system's default time zone is Asia/Shanghai, which is 8 hours ahead of UTC. Therefore, when we parse the date strings into <code>Date</code> objects, the time is interpreted as 23:54:07 and 23:54:08 in the system's time zone (which is 8 hours ahead of UTC), and the resulting epoch-milli times are 353 milliseconds apart.</p>
<p>On the other hand, if we change the dates to times 1 second later:</p>
<pre><code>String str3 = &quot;1927-12-31 23:54:08&quot;;  
String str4 = &quot;1927-12-31 23:54:09&quot;;  
</code></pre>
<p>Then the resulting epoch-milli times will be the same (1 second apart), and the subtraction will give the expected result of 1.</p>
<p>To fix the issue and get the expected result of 1, you can either:</p>
<ul>
<li>Set the default time zone to UTC when parsing the date strings:</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>SimpleDateFormat sf = new SimpleDateFormat(&quot;yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss&quot;);  
sf.setTimeZone(TimeZone.getTimeZone(&quot;UTC&quot;));  
Date sDt3 = sf.parse(str3);  
Date sDt4 = sf.parse(str4);
</code></pre>
<ul>
<li>Use a different date format that does not include the time zone information, such as:</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>SimpleDateFormat sf = new SimpleDateFormat(&quot;yyyy-MM-dd&quot;);  
sf.setTimeZone(TimeZone.getTimeZone(&quot;UTC&quot;));  
Date sDt3 = sf.parse(str3);  
Date sDt4 = sf.parse(str4);
</code></pre>
<p>By doing either of these, you can ensure that the date strings are interpreted as UTC times when parsing, and the resulting epoch-milli times will be the same (1 second apart), and the subtraction will give the expected result of 1.</p>
<p>Here is an updated version of your code that uses the first approach:</p>
<pre><code>import java.text.ParseException;  
import java.text.SimpleDateFormat;  
import java.util.Date;  
import java.util.TimeZone;  

public class Main {  
    public static void main(String[] args) throws ParseException {  
        SimpleDateFormat sf = new SimpleDateFormat(&quot;yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss&quot;);  
        sf.setTimeZone(TimeZone.getTimeZone(&quot;UTC&quot;));  
        String str3 = &quot;1927-12-31 23:54:07&quot;;  
        String str4 = &quot;1927-12-31 23:54:08&quot;;  
        Date sDt3 = sf.parse(str3);  
        Date sDt4 = sf.parse(str4);  
        long ld3 = sDt3.getTime() /1000;  
        long ld4 = sDt4.getTime() /1000;  
        System.out.println(ld4-ld3);  
    }  
}
</code></pre>
<p>When you run this updated code, the output will be:</p>
<pre><code>1
</code></pre>
<p>As expected, the subtraction of the two epoch-milli times gives the result of 1, which represents the one-second difference in the times.</p>

Certainly! To compare two dates in JavaScript, you can use the `Date` object and its methods. Here's an example of how you can compare two dates and check if they are greater than, less than, or not in the past:
```python
date1 = new Date('2022-05-01');
date2 = new Date('2022-05-03');

if (date1 > date2) {
  console.log('Date 1 is greater than Date 2');
} else if (date1 < date2) {
  console.log('Date 1 is less than Date 2');
} else {
  console.log('Date 1 and Date 2 are equal');
}
```
In this example, we create two `Date` objects (`date1` and `date2`) and compare them using the greater than (`>`), less than (`<`), and equal (`==`) operators. If `date1` is greater than `date2`, it will log 'Date 1 is greater than Date 2'. If `date1` is less than `date2`, it will log 'Date 1 is less than Date 2'. If `date1` and `date2` are equal, it will log 'Date 1 and Date 2 are equal'.
You can modify the example to compare dates coming from text boxes by converting the text box values to `Date` objects using the `new Date()` constructor. Here's an example:
```python
const textBox1 = document.getElementById('date1');
const textBox2 = document.getElementById('date2');

const date1 = new Date(textBox1.value);
const date2 = new Date(textBox2.value);

if (date1 > date2) {
  console.log('Date 1 is greater than Date 2');
} else if (date1 < date2) {
  console.log('Date 1 is less than Date 2');
} else {
  console.log('Date 1 and Date 2 are equal');
}
```
In this example, we get the values from the two text boxes (`textBox1` and `textBox2`) and convert them to `Date` objects using the `new Date()` constructor. We then compare the dates as before.
You can modify the example to check if the dates are in the past or not by adding additional conditions. Here's an example:
```python
const textBox1 = document.getElementById('date1');
const textBox2 = document.getElementById('date2');

const date1 = new Date(textBox1.value);
const date2 = new Date(textBox2.value);

if (date1 > date2) {
  console.log('Date 1 is greater than Date 2');
} else if (date1 < date2) {
  console.log('Date 1 is less than Date 2');
} else {
  console.log('Date 1 and Date 2 are equal');
}

if (date1 < new Date()) {
  console.log('Date 1 is in the past');
} else {
  console.log('Date 1 is in the future');
}

if (date2 < new Date()) {
  console.log('Date 2 is in the past');
} else {
  console.log('Date 2 is in the future');
}
```
In this example, we check if `date1` and `date2` are in the past or not by comparing them to the current date using the `<` operator. If `date1` is less than the current date, it will log 'Date 1 is in the past'. If `date1` is greater than or equal to the current date, it will log 'Date 1 is in the future'. The same logic is applied to `date2`.
I hope this helps! Let me know if you have any questions.

Certainly! The `Date` object in JavaScript provides several methods for formatting dates and times. Here are some commonly used methods and format specifiers:

### Formatting Dates Using `Date` Methods

#### `toLocaleDateString()`
This method returns a string with a language sensitive representation of the date portion of this date. You can specify the locale and options.

```javascript
const date = new Date();
console.log(date.toLocaleDateString('en-US', { year: 'numeric', month: 'long', day: 'numeric' }));
// Output: June 29, 2023
```

#### `toLocaleTimeString()`
This method returns a string with a language sensitive representation of the time portion of this date. You can specify the locale and options.

```javascript
const date = new Date();
console.log(date.toLocaleTimeString('en-US', { hour: 'numeric', minute: 'numeric', second: 'numeric' }));
// Output: 11:20:30 AM
```

#### `toString()`
While `toString()` is not typically used for formatting dates, it can be useful for debugging purposes. It returns a string representing the date and time.

```javascript
const date = new Date();
console.log(date.toString());
// Output: Mon Jun 29 2023 11:20:30 GMT-0700 (Pacific Daylight Time)
```

### Custom Formatting Using `Date.prototype.toLocaleString()`

You can create custom formatting by combining the above methods or using the `toLocaleString()` method directly.

```javascript
const date = new Date();
console.log(date.toLocaleString('en-US', { year: 'numeric', month: 'long', day: 'numeric', hour: 'numeric', minute: 'numeric', second: 'numeric' }));
// Output: June 29, 2023, 11:20:30 AM
```

### Using Moment.js Library
If you need more advanced date formatting, you might consider using a library like Moment.js. Here’s an example:

```javascript
// Install Moment.js via npm if not already installed
// npm install moment

import moment from 'moment';

const date = moment().format('YYYY-MM-DD HH:mm:ss');
console.log(date);
// Output: 2023-06-29 11:20:30
```

### Example Usage
Here’s how you can use these methods in practice:

```javascript
const date = new Date();

// Using toLocaleDateString()
console.log(date.toLocaleDateString('en-US', { year: 'numeric', month: 'long', day: 'numeric' }));

// Using toLocaleTimeString()
console.log(date.toLocaleTimeString('en-US', { hour: 'numeric', minute: 'numeric', second: 'numeric' }));

// Using toLocaleString() with custom options
console.log(date.toLocaleString('en-US', { year: 'numeric', month: 'long', day: 'numeric', hour: 'numeric', minute: 'numeric', second: 'numeric' }));
```

These examples should help you get started with formatting dates and times in JavaScript. If you have any specific requirements or need further assistance, feel free to ask!

