Title: How to validate an e-mail address in swift?.
Body: <p>Does anyone know how to validate an e-mail address in Swift? I found this code:</p>

<pre><code>- (BOOL) validEmail:(NSString*) emailString {

    if([emailString length]==0){
        return NO;
    }

    NSString *regExPattern = @"[A-Z0-9a-z._%+-]+@[A-Za-z0-9.-]+\\.[A-Za-z]{2,4}";

    NSRegularExpression *regEx = [[NSRegularExpression alloc] initWithPattern:regExPattern options:NSRegularExpressionCaseInsensitive error:nil];
    NSUInteger regExMatches = [regEx numberOfMatchesInString:emailString options:0 range:NSMakeRange(0, [emailString length])];

    NSLog(@"%i", regExMatches);
    if (regExMatches == 0) {
        return NO;
    } else {
        return YES;
    }
}
</code></pre>

<p>but I can't translate it to Swift.</p>

Answers:
Certainly! Here's how you can translate the provided Objective-C code into Swift:

<pre><code>import Foundation

func isValidEmail(_ email: String) -> Bool {
    let emailRegEx = "^[A-Z0-9a-z._%+-]+@[A-Za-z0-9.-]+\\.[A-Za-z]{2,4}$"
    let emailTest = NSPredicate(format: "SELF MATCHES %@", emailRegEx)
    return emailTest.evaluate(with: email)
}
</code></pre>

This Swift function uses the `NSPredicate` class to perform the email validation by matching against the regular expression pattern. The `isValidEmail` function takes an email string as input and returns a boolean indicating whether the email is valid or not.

You can call this function like this:

<pre><code>let email = "example@example.com"
if isValidEmail(email) {
    print("Valid email")
} else {
    print("Invalid email")
}
</code></pre>

This will print "Valid email" if the provided email is valid according to the regular expression pattern, and "Invalid email" otherwise. 

Note that Swift uses the `NSRegularExpression` class instead of `NSPredicate` for matching regular expressions, and the syntax for creating a regular expression pattern is slightly different from Objective-C. 

Additionally, you may want to add more validation checks or error handling depending on your specific requirements. 

