Title: Sorting object property by values.
Body: <p>If I have a JavaScript object such as:</p>

<pre><code>var list = {
  "you": 100, 
  "me": 75, 
  "foo": 116, 
  "bar": 15
};
</code></pre>

<p>Is there a way to sort the properties based on value? So that I end up with</p>

<pre><code>list = {
  "bar": 15, 
  "me": 75, 
  "you": 100, 
  "foo": 116
};
</code></pre>

Answers:
Certainly! You can sort the properties of an object by their corresponding values using JavaScript. Here's how you can achieve this:

```javascript
// Define the original object
var list = {
  "you": 100,
  "me": 75,
  "foo": 116,
  "bar": 15
};

// Convert the object into an array of [key, value] pairs
var entries = Object.entries(list);

// Sort the array based on the second element of each pair (the value)
entries.sort((a, b) => a[1] - b[1]);

// Convert the sorted array back into an object
list = Object.fromEntries(entries);

console.log(list);
```

This will output:

```javascript
{
  bar: 15,
  me: 75,
  you: 100,
  foo: 116
}
```

Explanation:
1. `Object.entries(list)` converts the object into an array of key-value pairs.
2. `entries.sort((a, b) => a[1] - b[1])` sorts the array based on the second element of each pair (the value). The comparison function `(a, b) => a[1] - b[1]` ensures that the sorting is done numerically.
3. `Object.fromEntries(entries)` converts the sorted array back into an object.

This approach works well for simple objects where the keys are strings and the values are numbers. If you need more complex sorting or different data types, you may need to adjust the sorting logic accordingly.

