Title: How do I space out the child elements of a StackPanel?.
Body: <p>Given a StackPanel:</p>

<pre><code>&lt;StackPanel&gt;
  &lt;TextBox Height="30"&gt;Apple&lt;/TextBox&gt;
  &lt;TextBox Height="80"&gt;Banana&lt;/TextBox&gt;
  &lt;TextBox Height="120"&gt;Cherry&lt;/TextBox&gt;
&lt;/StackPanel&gt;
</code></pre>

<p>What's the best way to space out the child elements so that there are equally-sized gaps between them, even though the child elements themselves are of different sizes? Can it be done without setting properties on each of the individual children?</p>

Answers:
You can use the `VerticalAlignment` property on the StackPanel to align the children vertically and the `HorizontalAlignment` property to align the children horizontally. Then, you can set the `Margin` property on the StackPanel to add space between the children. 

Here's an example of how you can do this:

<pre><code>&lt;StackPanel VerticalAlignment="Center" HorizontalAlignment="Center" Margin="10"&gt;
  &lt;TextBox Height="30"&gt;Apple&lt;/TextBox&gt;
  &lt;TextBox Height="80"&gt;Banana&lt;/TextBox&gt;
  &lt;TextBox Height="120"&gt;Cherry&lt;/TextBox&gt;
&lt;/StackPanel&gt;
</code></pre>

This will center the StackPanel on the screen and add a 10-pixel margin between each child element.
