Certainly! To pass an enum value as a command parameter in WPF, you can use a converter to convert the enum value to an object that can be used as a command parameter. Here's an example of how you can do this:

<pre><code>public class EnumToCommandParameterConverter : IValueConverter
{
    public object Convert(object value, Type targetType, object parameter, CultureInfo culture)
    {
        return value;
    }

    public object ConvertBack(object value, Type targetType, object parameter, CultureInfo culture)
    {
        return value;
    }
}
</code></pre>

<p>Then, in your XAML, you can use the converter to convert the enum value to an object that can be used as a command parameter:</p>

<pre><code>&lt;Window.Resources&gt;
    &lt;local:EnumToCommandParameterConverter x:Key="EnumToCommandParameterConverter" /&gt;
&lt;/Window.Resources&gt;

&lt;Button 
    x:Name="uxSearchButton" 
    Command="{Binding Path=SearchMembersCommand}" 
    CommandParameter="{Binding Converter={StaticResource EnumToCommandParameterConverter}, Path=SearchPageType.First}"
    Content="Search"&gt;
&lt;/Button&gt;
</code></pre>

<p>This will pass the enum value as a command parameter to your command.</p>

<p>Note that this is just one way to pass an enum value as a command parameter. You can also use a different approach, such as using a custom converter or a different binding mechanism.</p>
