To validate email addresses using JQuery, you can use a regular expression (regex) pattern to check if the input string matches the email format. Here's an example of how you can do it:</p>
<pre><code>$(document).ready(function() {
  $("#email").on("blur", function() {
    var email = $(this).val();
    var emailRegex = /^[^\s@]+@[^\s@]+\.[^\s@]+$/;
    if (!emailRegex.test(email)) {
      $(this).addClass("error");
      alert("Invalid email address");
    } else {
      $(this).removeClass("error");
    }
  });
});</code></pre>
In this example, we're using the <code>on("blur", function())</code> event to listen for the "blur" event on the email input field. This event is triggered when the input field loses focus. We then retrieve the value of the email input field using the <code>val()</code> method and store it in the <code>email</code> variable. Next, we define a regex pattern using the <code>emailRegex</code> variable that matches the email format. We use the <code>test()</code> method of the regex pattern to check if the email address matches the pattern. If it doesn't match, we add the <code>error</code> class to the input field and display an alert message. Otherwise, we remove the <code>error</code> class from the input field.
You can customize the regex pattern to match the email format that you want to validate. You can also customize the alert message to display a more informative error message.
This is just a basic example of how you can use JQuery to validate email addresses. You can further customize and enhance the code to meet your specific requirements.
